me(13195): range(2, 13194), i=2: 13195%2=1; i=3: 13195%3 = 13195 - 3*4398=13194 \u2192 1; i=4: 13195%4=3; i=5: 0 \u2192 return False. Quick. \u2713\n\nTotal work: outer loop 13194 iterations of n%j \u2014 trivial. Good.\n\nLet me finalize. Also consider adding an assertion message? Not necessary.\n\nDo I need `import f059`? Only if patching module attr. I chose builtins patch, so no need. But hmm \u2014 should I ALSO patch f059 module globals as a belt-and-braces? If f059's globals somehow contain range... they don't. Keep it simple: builtins patch.\n\nWait, actually, one concern with patching builtins.range: pytest's assertion rewriting / error reporting uses range? Only on failures. Our test passes. And monkeypatch restores before teardown checks. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider: is there any chance the grader runs tests with `-p no:monkeypatch`? Unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, avoid monkeypatch entirely and skip line 5? The instruction explicitly says to execute ALL missing lines and branches. I'll keep the patch test.\n\nOne more thought \u2014 maybe instead of patching builtins.range globally, patch `f059.range` via monkeypatch.setattr(f059, \"range\", fake_range, raising=False). This is strictly more surgical: only code in f059's module namespace sees the fake. If f059.__init__ has other functions using range, they'd also see it during the test \u2014 but we don't call them. I think module-level patch is cleaner and safer for state pollution. But it requires `import f059`. And if the harness imports the function differently (e.g., `from f059 import largest_prime_factor` \u2014 same module object). Fine.\n\nHmm, but what if the harness places the code at a different module path? The task explicitly says f059/__init__.py. Both `import f059` and `from f059 import largest_prime_factor` should work.\n\nDecision: use module-level patch with raising=False, AND fall back... no, keep one approach. Actually, let me think once more: which is more likely to work