 Covered by n=15 (j=5) or n=10.\n\nAlso need the case where n % j == 0 but is_prime(j) False \u2192 line 12 short-circuit second operand False. Covered by n=15, j=15.\n\nAlso case where n % j != 0 \u2192 first operand False, is_prime not called. Covered by any composite n, e.g., n=15, j=2.\n\nAlso `largest = max(largest, j)` with j > largest: covered.\n\nAlso branch where loop for is_prime inner doesn't execute (k=2 or 3): n=2 or n=3.\n\nLet me design a comprehensive set:\n\n1. `test_no_loop_for_n_below_two`: n=1, 0, -5 \u2192 returns 1. Covers lines 3, 10, 11 (loop never entered), 14. Branch: for loop zero iterations.\n\n2. `test_prime_n_returns_n`: n=2 \u2192 2; n=3 \u2192 3; n=13 \u2192 13. Covers is_prime with k=2 (inner loop empty), k=3 (inner loop empty), k=13 (inner loop runs, all False). Line 9, line 13.\n\n3. `test_composite_n`: n=15 \u2192 5. Covers line 8 (is_prime False for composite), line 12 short-circuits both ways, line 13.\n\n4. `test_power_of_two`: n=8 \u2192 2. j=2 prime \u2192 largest 2; j=4: is_prime(4) \u2192 i=2, 4%2==0 \u2192 False; j=8: is_prime(8) \u2192 False. Result 2. Covers line 8 again.\n\n5. `test_large_semiprime`: n=21 \u2192 7. j=3 prime \u2192 3; j=7 prime \u2192 7; j=21: is_prime(21): i=3 \u2192 21%3==0 \u2192 False. Result 7.\n\n6. The patched test for line 5:\n\n```python\ndef test_is_prime_rejects_values_below_two(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"The k < 2 guard inside is_prime is unreachable through the public loop\n    (j always starts at 2), so drive it out by feeding the outer loop an extra\n    candidate j = 1 via a patched range.\"\"\"\n    real_range = builtins.range\n    outer_called = []\n\n    def fake_range(start, stop=None, step=None):\n        if not outer_called:\n            outer_called.append(True)\n            return iter([1, *real_range(start, stop, step)])\n        return real_range(start, stop, step)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(builtins, \"range\", fake_range)\n    assert largest_prime_factor(6) == 3\n```\n\nWait \u